WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.867
Preview: The guest changed the order. Which version is the kitchen making?

2
00:00:04.867 --> 00:00:12.600
Myke Mueller. Never86’d founder. Community Tap & Pizza operator.

3
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:26.867
Two crew pickups. One revised request. Keep the original message in view.

4
00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:32.900
Muse checked the restaurant’s facts before drafting.

5
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:40.300
Muse left the pickup location blank. The guest had already supplied it.

6
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:50.867
Record the location and reason. Approval comes next.

7
00:00:50.867 --> 00:00:59.100
Twelve pizzas. Two pickups. A $198 quote for this draft.

8
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:08.000
Review the price, pickup times and capacity before saying yes.

9
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:14.567
Two simulated receipts: five pizzas first, seven later.

10
00:01:14.567 --> 00:01:23.967
One more garden pizza. This change needs a new decision.

11
00:01:23.967 --> 00:01:33.800
Review the revised $215 quote. Ask the kitchen to acknowledge the change.

12
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:42.333
Current receipts: five and eight. Earlier versions stay in history.

13
00:01:42.333 --> 00:01:54.500
A different case: November 4. The operator reviews a pickup only rule.

14
00:01:54.500 --> 00:02:03.467
After a real restart, the reviewed rule returns. Demo orders reset.

15
00:02:03.467 --> 00:02:10.800
Three checks keep the rule with this restaurant and date.

16
00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:19.167
The next guest requested delivery. The saved review keeps that request intact.

17
00:02:19.167 --> 00:02:29.700
The reply overstates the reason. The operator edits it and keeps it unsent.

18
00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:37.667
The guest must accept pickup before the delivery request can change.

19
00:02:37.667 --> 00:02:45.467
The detail. The decision. Who checked it.
